  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "The initial velocity of a particle is $(2i+5j)\\,\\text{m/s}.$ Find the **exact** speed of the particle after $3$ seconds if the particle moves with a constant acceleration of $(-3i+j)\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\n$|v|=$\n\n{{field-1}} $\\text{m/s}.$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nv=u+at,\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is the initial velocity, and $a$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nHere, we have\n\n$$\nu=2i+5j,\\,a=-3i+j.\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $t=3\\,\\text{s},$ the velocity of the particle will be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\  & =(2i+5j)+(-3i+j)(3) \\\\  & =(2i+5j)+(-9i+3j) \\\\  & =(-7\\,i+8\\,j)\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the speed of the particle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|v| & =|-7i+8j| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-7)^{2}+{8}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49+64} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{113}\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
